17592186045417] [17592186284173 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284173 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284173 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284173 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284173 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bf6386c-b8c7-53b9-abbf-517dbee91f42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1071 "The projection of the intersection between the parabolic cylinder $4(x-3)^{2}=12(z+1)$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of the parabola?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d45d5923-cf19-557a-b6bb-276b79a4af56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284485 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $y=2,$ we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $y$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2} & =12(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3(z+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (z+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane with the vertex at $(3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12ff0311-caaa-5591-a831-5519fc8ddee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284489 #edb/ref 1074 "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(x+3)^{2}=6y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c06a8e8-6e3d-50f3-ae2e-200f38471a76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401467 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =6y \\\\ 9 & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a line in the $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401468 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "770068c0-e